{"name":"Flipper","hex":"#7f726b","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"Flipper","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Proper noun","definitions":["A common name for aquatic animals which have flippers, such as dolphins and seals."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/Flipper"}],"entries":[{"title":"Flipper (anatomy)","blocks":[{"text":"A flipper is a broad, flattened limb adapted for aquatic locomotion. It refers to the fully webbed, swimming appendages of aquatic vertebrates that are not fish."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/07/Schwimmender-Pinguin.jpg/330px-Schwimmender-Pinguin.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Flipper_(anatomy)"},{"title":"Swimfin","blocks":[{"text":"Swimfins, swim fins, diving fins, or flippers are finlike accessories worn on the feet, legs or hands and made from rubber, plastic, carbon fiber or combinations of these materials, to aid movement through the water in water sports activities such as swimming, bodyboarding, bodysurfing, float-tube fishing, kneeboarding, riverboarding, scuba diving, snorkeling, spearfishing, underwater hockey, underwater rugby and various other types of underwater diving."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b8/SwimFins_02.jpg/330px-SwimFins_02.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Swimfin"},{"title":"Flipper (band)","blocks":[{"text":"Flipper is an American punk rock band formed in San Francisco, California in 1979. The band influenced a number of grunge, punk rock and noise rock bands. Their slowed-down, bass-driven and heavily distorted style of punk is considered to have inspired bands such as the Melvins and Nirvana, whose bass player Krist Novoselic played with the band in the 2000s."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/68/Flipper_930club.jpg/330px-Flipper_930club.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Flipper_(band)"},{"title":"Flipper (1964 TV series)","blocks":[{"text":"Flipper is an American television program broadcast on NBC from September 19, 1964, until April 15, 1967. Flipper, a bottlenose dolphin, is the pet of Porter Ricks, chief warden at Coral Key Park and Marine Preserve, and his two young sons, Sandy and Bud. The show has been dubbed an \"aquatic Lassie\", and a considerable amount of children's merchandise inspired by the show was produced during its first run."}],"image":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/d/d1/Flipper_Title_Screen.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Flipper_(1964_TV_series)"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/86/Chelonia_mydas_is_going_for_the_air.jpg/500px-Chelonia_mydas_is_going_for_the_air.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/86/Chelonia_mydas_is_going_for_the_air.jpg/1280px-Chelonia_mydas_is_going_for_the_air.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/86/Chelonia_mydas_is_going_for_the_air.jpg/500px-Chelonia_mydas_is_going_for_the_air.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"This green turtle is about to break the surface for air at Kona, Hawaii.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AChelonia_mydas_is_going_for_the_air.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/ca/Humpback_stellwagen.JPG/500px-Humpback_stellwagen.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/ca/Humpback_stellwagen.JPG/1280px-Humpback_stellwagen.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/ca/Humpback_stellwagen.JPG/500px-Humpback_stellwagen.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"The tubercles on the flippers of humpback whales improve the hydrodynamics of the flipper at their size. Breaking up channels of fast-moving water allows humpbacks to retain their \"grip\" on the water, and turn at sharper angles even at low velocities.[9]","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AHumpback_stellwagen.JPG"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/ca/Flipper_eines_Finnwales.jpg/500px-Flipper_eines_Finnwales.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/ca/Flipper_eines_Finnwales.jpg/500px-Flipper_eines_Finnwales.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Close up skeletal of fin whale flipper","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AFlipper_eines_Finnwales.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/7d/The_Osteology_of_the_Reptiles_p193.png/500px-The_Osteology_of_the_Reptiles_p193.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/7d/The_Osteology_of_the_Reptiles_p193.png/1280px-The_Osteology_of_the_Reptiles_p193.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/7d/The_Osteology_of_the_Reptiles_p193.png/500px-The_Osteology_of_the_Reptiles_p193.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Illustration of limbs of aquatic reptiles: the mosasaurs Platecarpus and Clidastes, and the ichthyosaurs Ophthalmosaurus and Platypterygius. From The Osteology of the Reptiles (1925)","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AT